      "risk": "Task 66 demonstrates the passing counterpart: the agent explained that a pending item could not be returned, explicitly offered cancellation of the entire order, and obtained the user's agreement to that expanded scope. Preserve that supported path rather than rejecting all cancellation requests involving individual items. Task 8 demonstrates successful scope narrowing: when the user deferred the water bottle, the agent exchanged only the desk lamp and left other items untouched. Preserve selective exchanges, aggregation of exchanges within each order, and valid exchange refunds to the user's chosen card. Repeated confirmations already add interaction cost in all three trajectories; additional confirmation alone would not resolve a misleading action description. Mixed text/tool messages and multiple simultaneous calls also occur in passing cases, with protocol enforcement disabled and zero reported hard violations, so they do not establish the cause of the native reward failure. No previous mutation effects are available, and the opaque evaluation does not establish whether the cancellation scope mismatch fully explains the zero reward."
